    "custom_fields" : {
      "Data Quality" : {
        "Geospatial Resolution" : "National",
        "Footnotes" : "Note: Data from prior seasons have been archived and can be accessed using the following link: (https://www.cdc.gov/rsvvaxview/dashboard/2023-24-pregnant-women-coverage.html?CDC_AA_refVal=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.cdc.gov%2Frsvvaxview%2Fdashboard%2F2023-24-pregnant-persons-coverage.html). Achived 2023–24 season data are based on 8 reporting sites whereas data reported during 202–25 season and on this dashboard include 10 reporting sites. Naleway AL, Crane B, Irving SA, et al. Vaccine Safety Datalink infrastructure enhancements for evaluating the safety of maternal vaccination. Ther Adv Drug Saf. 2021, Jun 14 (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8207278/pdf/10.1177_20420986211021233.pdf). Pregnant women who were past the recommended window of 32-36 week's gestation prior to September 1, 2025, were removed from the denominator. The denominator may include pregnant women 32–36 weeks of pregnancy as of September 1, 2025, or later, who received an RSV vaccine during a previous pregnancy. These pregnant women are therefore not eligible for another RSV vaccine this season/pregnancy; however, their infants would be eligible to receive nirsevimab (https://www.cdc.gov/vaccines/hcp/by-disease/rsv.html?CDC_AA_refVal=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.cdc.gov%2Fvaccines%2Fvpd%2Frsv%2Fhcp%2Fchild.html). Vaccinations may have been administered outside the recommended window of 32–36 weeks gestation (https://www.cdc.gov/rsv/hcp/vaccine-clinical-guidance/pregnant-people.html#:~:text=CDC%20recommends%20one%20dose%20of,and%20the%20U.S.%20Virgin%20Islands). The coverage estimate for any given week may change as the season progresses. Estimates presented here might underestimate RSV vaccination coverage among women enrolled in the VSD sites. Additional information on weekly estimation methodology vs. post-season estimation methodology can be found here: (https://www.cdc.gov/respvaxview/publications/pregnancy-coverage-estimation.html).",
        "Suggested Citation" : "National Center for Immunization and Respiratory Diseases, US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Weekly Percent of Pregnant Women Ageas 18-49 Years Vaccinated with RSV Vaccine Overall and by Race and Ethnicity, United States"
